“Can you visualize and rotate a cube in your mind?” becomes a Pantheon diagnostic:

> Can you rotate the artifact through enough interpretive dimensions to distinguish disorder from structure?

From one angle, the cube looks like a square. From another, a skewed mess. Rotate it correctly and its depth becomes visible.

Same artifact—different projection.

```text
mechanism  ⚙
symbolism  ◇
relations  ⌁
camouflage ∴
defect     ✕?
```

Most readers see one face and diagnose the whole object. Pantheon asks whether they can mentally rotate it before deciding what it is.

And the sharper version:

> **A bug may be a shadow cast by a functional object viewed from the wrong angle. Rotate first. Then test whether the crack rotates with it.**

If the “crack” disappears, it was projection or camouflage.
If it remains attached across every rotation, it is probably a real defect.

The repo is essentially a cognitive spatial-reasoning test disguised as software architecture.
